---

# 17. Non-Functional Business Expectations

The system is expected to provide:

### Data Integrity

HR records should maintain relational consistency and controlled values.

### Availability

The application should be available to authorized users during expected operational usage.

### Maintainability

The system should be modular so that additional HR functionality can be introduced without redesigning the entire solution.

### Scalability

The data model should support growth in employee, attendance, recruitment and training records.

### Security

Credentials and sensitive configuration values must not be stored in source code or documentation.

### Traceability

Business requirements should be traceable through functional requirements, implementation and testing.

---

# 18. Reporting and KPI Expectations

Management reporting should support:

* Total employees
* Department employee count
* Active workforce
* Employment status distribution
* Attendance distribution
* Leave activity
* Performance averages
* PIP population
* Training completion
* Training scores
* Recruitment pipeline
* Interview activity
* Offer activity

KPI definitions will be formally documented in the Analytics/BI section.

---

# 19. Assumptions

1. HR business stakeholders will validate business requirements.
2. Business users will participate in UAT.
3. PostgreSQL will remain the primary transactional database.
4. Business rules will be documented before being considered baselined.
5. Power BI will use approved HR360 data outputs.
6. Users will access the system through supported web browsers.

---

# 20. Dependencies

The project depends on:

* PostgreSQL availability
* Backend API availability
* Frontend/API integration
* Accurate master data
* Stakeholder validation
* Business-rule approval
* UAT participation
* Power BI data mapping for future BI implementation

---

# 21. Key Business Risks

| Risk                           | Business Impact                 |
| ------------------------------ | ------------------------------- |
| Inconsistent employee data     | Incorrect HR reporting          |
| Incorrect active-status logic  | Incorrect workforce visibility  |
| Incomplete recruitment records | Poor hiring pipeline visibility |
| Inconsistent KPI definitions   | Conflicting management reports  |
| Delayed stakeholder validation | Sprint delays                   |
| Insufficient UAT               | Production defects              |

---

# 22. Success Measures

The business solution will be considered successful when:

* Employee information is centralized.
* Core HR processes are represented digitally.
* Department-specific business rules operate correctly.
* HR users can access required operational information.
* Management can obtain defined HR KPIs.
* Recruitment pipeline information is traceable.
* Training and performance information can be analyzed.
* Requirements can be traced to testing and acceptance.

---

